<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russian Revolution of 1905</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1905</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Revolution of 1905</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russian Revolution of 1905 was a wave of strikes, peasant uprisings, mutinies and political protest across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, sparked by the Bloody Sunday massacre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> and defeat in the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> to issue the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-manifesto">October Manifesto</a>, which granted civil liberties and created an elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-duma">State Duma</a>. The Russian Empire profile presents it as a sign of the deep instability that culminated in the revolutions of 1917.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905.jpg" alt="Russian Revolution of 1905" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wojciech Kossak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloody_Sunday_in_1905_by_Wojciech_Kossak.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
